## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limit of a sequence)
## Date(09/16/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'limit of a sequence')

###########################
#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "PGcourse.pl");

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => "Real");

$a = random(2, 5, 1);
$b = random(2, 5, 1);

BEGIN_PGML

Find the limit of the sequence [``\{a_n\} = \{\frac{[$a]n}{[$b]^n}\}``].



[``\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n=``][_]{Real(0)}{10}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
If we let [`` f(x) = \frac{[$a]x}{[$b]^x} ``], then

[```
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n & = \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) \\
& = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{[$a]x}{[$b]^x} \qquad \text{using L'Hopital's Rule} \\
& = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{[$a]}{[$b]^x \ln([$b])} = 0
\end{aligned}
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
